INT. COURTROOM 4B - SPLIT-LEVEL ANNEX - DAY

Wood panelling, fluorescent hum. A cracked window shows a parking structure. The room is packed and silent.

JUDGE EDITH WHITLOCK (60s, silver bob, half-moon glasses) presides. At the plaintiff's table, ATTORNEY VICTOR DELANEY (40s, three-piece suit) shuffles notes beside his client, ROSALIND PEARCE (50s, nurse's cardigan, wedding ring she keeps turning). At the defence table, ATTORNEY FELIX OYELOWO (30s, immaculate) sits beside his client, DR. NATHANIEL STROUD (60s, surgeon, bow tie). In the box, a nervous BAILIFF HUGO (30s, uniform too big) stands at attention.

JUDGE WHITLOCK
Counsel, approach.

Delaney and Oyelowo rise together, stride to the bench. She leans down, eyes narrowing.

JUDGE WHITLOCK (CONT'D)
I will not have another outburst. Is that clear?

DELANEY
Perfectly, Your Honour.

OYELOWO ^
Perfectly, Your Honour.

She sits back. They return to their tables. Delaney drops a folder in front of Rosalind, who squares it with both hands. Stroud leans toward Oyelowo and whispers; Oyelowo shakes his head and stares straight ahead.

DELANEY
Mrs. Pearce, tell the court what you saw in the operating room.

ROSALIND
I saw the anaesthesia log. It was blank.

OYELOWO
Objection. Foundation.

JUDGE WHITLOCK
Overruled. Continue.

ROSALIND
It was blank for forty minutes.

DELANEY
And who was responsible for completing that log?

ROSALIND
Dr. Stroud.

A murmur from the gallery. Stroud's jaw tightens. He lifts a pen, sets it down, lifts it again. Hugo shifts his weight and clears his throat.

OYELOWO
Mrs. Pearce, you were the circulating nurse that day?

ROSALIND
I was.

OYELOWO
And the log was your responsibility as well.

ROSALIND
It was not.

OYELOWO
You're sure.

ROSALIND
I've worked in that theatre for twenty-two years. I'm sure.

Oyelowo studies her. She holds his gaze. Delaney leans back, folding his hands, a small smile at the corner of his mouth.

JUDGE WHITLOCK
Mr. Oyelowo, do you have a question?

OYELOWO
Several, Your Honour. But I'll start with one.

He turns, crosses to the box, stands very close.

OYELOWO (CONT'D)
Why did you wait eleven months to report it?

ROSALIND
Because no one asked.

Silence. Stroud finally looks up. For the first time, he looks at her.
